/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a binary mixture of A and B.
   - pA, pB are the partial pressures of A and B (kPa); xA is the liquid mole fraction of A and xB = 1 − xA; P°A, P°B are the pure-component vapour pressures at the same temperature (kPa); P is the total pressure (kPa).
   - Raoult's law gives pA from the liquid composition.
   - xA is liquid mole fraction of A; yA is vapour mole fraction of A; P is total pressure (kPa); P°A and P°B are pure-component vapour pressures (kPa).
   - At a fixed temperature, liquid and vapour can coexist only when P lies between P°B and P°A (A the more volatile).
   - Relative volatility αAB measures how easily A leaves B.
   - αAB is relative volatility of A with respect to B (dimensionless); yA, yB are vapour mole fractions; xA, xB are liquid mole fractions; P°A, P°B are pure-component vapour pressures at the same temperature (kPa).
   - If α > 1, A is more volatile and the vapour is richer in A.
   - Real mixtures depart from Raoult's law because unlike molecules attract or repel each other.
   - A real y-x curve can cross the diagonal.
*/
