/* book exam traps: Using the average distillate composition in place of the instantaneous equilibrium y. The Rayleigh integral needs y at each local x. | Reversing the limits x₁ and x₂ in the integral, or writing L₂/L₁ when the equation is L₁/L₂. | Forgetting that the constant-α formula fails at α = 1; there y = x and no separation occurs. | Treating steam distillation as ordinary distillation of a water–organic solution. It needs two immiscible liquid phases; a soluble solvent changes the vapour pressures. | Unit slip: α and mole fractions are dimensionless; L₁, L₂, D are moles, not mass, unless the same mass basis is used consistently. | Writing the steam ratio upside down. Moles of steam per mole of organic = p°water/p°organic; multiply by 18/Morganic for a mass ratio. */
